, 33, 70; it is listed as the eighth parvan in the list of the eighteen major parvans by those who have thought about Bhārata (bhāratacintakaiḥ) 1. 2. 169, 172, 233, 71.

Described as containing great wonders (paramādbhutam); it has 69 Adhyāyas and 4900 Ślokas 1. 2. 169, 172; in this parvan we are told the following:

(1) the Madrarāja (Śalya) was asked to act as a charioteer;

(2) an ancient ākhyāna about the destruction of the Tripuras;

(3) harsh dispute between Karṇa and Śalya;

(4) the story of the swan and the crow;

(5) Yudhiṣṭhira and Arjuna getting angry with each other;

(6) Karṇa's death at the hands of Arjuna in a duel that was fought from chariots (dvairatha) 1. 2. 169-171; the last incident is referred to in Dhṛtarāṣṭra's summary made for Saṁjaya 1. 1. 146.

